# request: "Walk me through what happens when a shopper opens a product page, for
#  the onboarding doc. The browser sends GET /products/42 to CloudFront; on a cache
#  miss CloudFront forwards it to the Kong API gateway in our AWS VPC. Kong routes
#  /products to the Catalog API; it also fronts the Orders and Cart APIs on /orders
#  and /cart. Each API is a Go service with its own Postgres database, and the
#  Catalog API selects product 42 from the catalog DB. The reply goes back the same
#  way and CloudFront caches it for 60 seconds. Number the hops and highlight the
#  Catalog API."
type: walkthrough
reader: new engineers reading the onboarding doc
width: 800
direction: down
focus: aws.catalog, browser -> cdn -> aws.gw -> aws.catalog -> aws.catalogdb  # "highlight the Catalog API"; "The browser sends GET /products/42 to CloudFront"
out: DNS and TLS handshakes, auth, the cache write-back, logging
nodes:
  browser: Browser
  reply: 5. The reply retraces the path;\nCloudFront caches it 60 s {note}
  cdn: CDN\nCloudFront
  aws: AWS VPC
  aws.gw: API gateway\nKong
  aws.orders: Orders API\nGo
  aws.catalog: Catalog API\nGo
  aws.cart: Cart API\nGo
  aws.ordersdb: Orders DB\nPostgres {shape: cylinder}
  aws.catalogdb: Catalog DB\nPostgres {shape: cylinder}
  aws.cartdb: Cart DB\nPostgres {shape: cylinder}
edges:
  browser -> cdn: 1. GET /products/42
  cdn -> aws.gw: 2. cache miss
  aws.gw -> aws.orders: /orders
  aws.gw -> aws.catalog: 3. route /products
  aws.gw -> aws.cart: /cart
  aws.orders -> aws.ordersdb: SQL {inferred}
  aws.catalog -> aws.catalogdb: 4. SELECT product 42
  aws.cart -> aws.cartdb: SQL {inferred}
